# Deliberately narrow. There is no `* text=auto' here: this repository has gone
# its whole life without a .gitattributes, every text file is committed with LF
# already, and a blanket rule would renormalise files nobody is looking at as a
# side effect of a change about icons.
#
# What is pinned is the artwork and its renders, because a check depends on
# their bytes.

# cmake/CheckIconStamp.cmake hashes this file to decide whether the committed
# .ico and .icns are still current. GitHub's Windows runners set core.autocrlf,
# so without this the checkout there differs byte-for-byte from the commit
# while describing exactly the same drawing -- which is how the check first
# failed on Windows for a file nobody had touched.
#
# The check normalises line endings before hashing, so this is the belt and
# that is the braces. Either alone would do; the reason for both is that they
# fail differently. This keeps working trees identical across platforms, but
# only after a fresh checkout or a `git add --renormalize'. The normalising
# hash needs neither, and covers a tree that predates this file.
*.svg text eol=lf

# Renders of the above, and never text. Git's own binary detection would reach
# the same conclusion from the NUL bytes; saying so means it is not left to a
# heuristic, and that a future blanket `text=auto' cannot corrupt them.
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary

# Generated by scripts/dspnodes and diffed against a fresh one by a ctest, so
# the bytes matter here for the same reason they matter above -- and it failed
# the same way, on Windows, reporting line 1 as different when both copies read
# `# Node reference'. The difference was the carriage return the checkout had
# added.
#
# dspnodes ignores carriage returns when it compares, so this is the belt and
# that is the braces, exactly as with the artwork. Either alone would do.
NODES.md text eol=lf

# What GitHub thinks this repository is written in, which without these two
# lines includes a language it has never used.
#
# linguist reads `.dsp' as Faust, which shares the extension and nothing else.
# These are thinklang -- this tree's own language for a graph of nodes, with
# its own scanner in libthink -- and 340 kB of them made Faust the third
# language on the repository page, ahead of CMake. There is no thinklang for
# linguist to be told instead, so they come out of the count rather than stay
# in it under another language's name. The `.gen' pieces are already uncounted:
# linguist has no mapping for that extension at all.
*.dsp linguist-detectable=false

# And the scanner itself, which is Flex. `.ll' is Flex's C++ suffix and also
# LLVM IR's, and linguist picks the latter.
*.ll linguist-language=Lex
